# Patterns here are matched against a file's whole path, so a leading `**/` is
# what makes one apply at any depth. Without it `node_modules` meant the root's
# alone, and `COPY . .` laid the host's `apps/online-backend/node_modules` over
# the tree `npm ci` had just installed in the image.
**/node_modules

# Every env file, at any depth. `.env.*` on its own matched neither a bare
# `.env` nor `apps/online-backend/.env` — which on a maintainer's machine holds
# the real BOT_CREDENTIALS, ADMIN_CREDENTIALS and DATABASE_URL, and which CI
# seeds from the sample right before building. Nothing in the image wants one:
# no build step reads an env file, and docker-compose.yml passes the backend its
# environment, which dotenv then leaves alone (#443).
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Real team lists are dropped into scripts/ on the host — that is why
# .gitignore carries `scripts/*.tsv*`. docker-compose.yml bind-mounts ./scripts
# in production too and `npm run teams:import` reads the file through that
# mount, so a TSV baked into the image would be competitors' data shipped for
# nothing.
scripts/*.tsv

# Build output, caches and history. Each is rebuilt or unused inside the image
# and only churns the `COPY . .` layer; apps/online-backend/dist is also what
# CMD runs, so host output landing there is a correctness hazard, not just noise.
.git
**/dist
**/build
**/.turbo
**/coverage
site

# The backend image has no use for the practice site, and copying it would
# invalidate the image cache on every practice change.
apps/strategy-practice
